.TH AGENTBOX 1 "2026-08-07" "agentbox 0.0.1-dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agentbox \- run an agentic coding tool inside an isolated, per-project environment
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agentbox
.RI [ "GLOBAL FLAGS" ]
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.RI [ ARGS... ]
.br
.B agentbox
.RI [ "GLOBAL FLAGS" ]
.B \-\-
.I COMMAND-IN-BOX
.RI [ ARGS... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agentbox
runs a command \(em typically an agentic coding tool such as
.B claude
\(em inside an isolated environment built for the current project, with no
per-project setup.
Invoking
.B agentbox claude
resolves the project root, merges configuration, selects a backend
satisfying the project's declared isolation floor, builds or pulls an image
matching the declared toolchains, constructs a masked view of the workspace
that hides secrets, starts a default-deny egress proxy, and executes the
agent with the working directory mapped to match the shell's.
.PP
If the first non-flag argument is not a reserved subcommand, it and
everything after it is treated as a command to run inside the box.
.B \-\-
forces that interpretation, so
.B "agentbox \-\- \-\-version"
runs
.B \-\-version
in the box.
With no arguments,
.B agentbox
runs
.IR [box].default_command ,
itself defaulting to an interactive login shell.
.PP
A project may hold several named
.IR boxes .
Bare
.B agentbox
attaches to the project's default box, creating one if the project has none.
See
.B "BOX SELECTION"
below.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.BI "run " CMD...
Explicit form of the default behavior: run
.I CMD
in the box.
.TP
.B shell
Interactive login shell in the box.
.TP
.BR new " [" \-\-name " \fIN\fR]"
Create an additional box in this project without attaching.
.TP
.BR attach " [" \-n " \fIN\fR]"
Attach to an existing box. Never creates one.
.TP
.BI "default " NAME
Set the project's default box.
.TP
.B up
Create and start the box; do not exec.
.TP
.B stop
Stop a box, retaining its state and tree, freeing guest memory.
.TP
.B down
Stop and remove the guest and networks; keep persistent state.
.TP
.B restart
Recreate the box, applying current configuration.
.TP
.BR status " [" \-\-all "] [" \-\-artifacts ]
Box state, active backend and the properties it enforces, drift, network
policy, and a mask summary. Always prints the box's instance name, tree
mode, backend, and isolation tier: with several boxes per project, "which
box am I looking at" is as easy to get wrong as "which tier am I getting".
.B \-\-all
covers every box in the project.
.IP
.B \-\-artifacts
adds everything agentbox is holding for the box: its container, networks and
home volume, its image, the host-side mask view and proxy listener under the
vm tier, its working tree and branch, and its state directory. Each is
resolved against what is actually there, so an artifact the box expects and
the engine does not hold reads
.BR MISSING ,
and one the box's name claims but that it was not created with reads
.BR unexpected .
What a box expects is taken from what it was created with, not from current
configuration \(em ordinary drift has its own line and is not an absence.
.BR "agentbox ls \-\-artifacts"
is the same view across boxes, and adds what nothing claims.
.TP
.B ps
Processes running in the box.
.TP
.BR logs " [" \-\-proxy "] [" \-\-denied "] [" \-f ]
Logs.
.B \-\-denied
filters the proxy log to refused requests \(em the answer to "what did the
agent try to reach".
.TP
.BR build " [" \-\-no\-cache ]
Build or rebuild the image.
.TP
.BR config " [" \-\-json "] [" \-\-origin ]
Effective merged configuration.
.B \-\-origin
annotates each key with the layer that set it.
.TP
.BR mounts " [" \-\-json ]
Every path presented to the guest, every mask, and the reason for each.
.TP
.BR masks " [" \-\-json "] [" \-\-verify ]
Effective mask set.
.B \-\-verify
asserts each mask is actually in force in a running box.
.TP
.BR backends " [" \-\-json ]
Available backends, their isolation tiers, and why any are unavailable.
.TP
.BI "allow " DOMAIN...
Append to the workspace config's allowlist. Rewrites the file structurally;
comments are not preserved (the command says so when it runs). Does not
invalidate recorded trust, since the edit was explicitly requested.
.TP
.BR bundles " [" \-\-list "] [" \-\-show " \fINAME\fR]"
Inspect built-in egress domain bundles. An opaque allowlist is not
auditable.
.TP
.BR init " [" \-\-profile " \fIP\fR]"
Scaffold
.B agentbox.toml
and
.BR .agentignore .
.TP
.BR ls " [" \-\-project "] [" \-\-all "] [" \-\-artifacts ]
Boxes grouped by project: instance name, backend, tier, tree mode, age,
resources. Boxes created with
.B \-\-force\-isolation
and boxes whose workspace no longer resolves are flagged. On its own
.B ls
contacts no engine, so it answers with the container daemon stopped.
.IP
.B \-\-artifacts
opts into the unified view: each box's live state, everything it is holding
(as described under
.BR status ),
and a closing list of every agentbox artifact on the host that no box in
agentbox state claims \(em always computed across all projects, since
"unclaimed" is only true when nothing claims it, and reclaimable with
.BR "agentbox prune" .
The two lists are the same join read in opposite directions: nothing appears
in both.
.TP
.BR rm " [" \-\-state "] [" \-\-delete\-branch "] [" \fIID\fR ]
Remove a box and its worktree.
.B \-\-state
also discards persistent guest state. The box's git branch is preserved
unless
.B \-\-delete\-branch
is given: unreviewed agent work is the most valuable thing in the box and
the easiest to destroy by accident.
.TP
.BR prune " [" \-\-apply "] [" \-\-images "] [" \-\-idle "] [" \-\-boxes "] [" \-\-running "] [" \-\-state ]
Report what agentbox has left behind and can reclaim: state for vanished
workspaces, engine containers, networks and volumes that no box in agentbox
state claims, and mask views with no box to serve. Only resources agentbox
itself would have created under that name are ever candidates.
.IP
Reports and removes nothing by default; the listing tells you what would go.
.B \-\-apply
reclaims it.
.B \-\-images
additionally considers built images no box references \(em they are excluded
otherwise because rebuilding one is expensive and superseding one is routine.
.B \-\-idle
includes boxes past their idle timeout, which are stopped, never removed.
Use
.B \-\-json
for the candidate set as data.
.IP
Live boxes are not candidates unless you say so.
.B \-\-boxes
widens the report to every
.I stopped
box in agentbox state, across all projects, and removes the guest, its
networks and its working tree.
.B \-\-running
additionally includes boxes that are running.
.B \-\-state
additionally discards each box's persistent home. Neither means anything on
its own: passing them without
.B \-\-boxes
is a usage error rather than a silent no-op. A box whose backend is
unavailable on this host cannot be inspected, and is left alone as though it
were running.
.IP
.B prune
never deletes a git branch, at any combination of flags: the working tree of
a removed box is gone, but the branch holding its unreviewed work survives.
Boxes it deliberately left alone are named at the end of the run, so a
partial teardown never reads as a complete one. The full teardown is
.BR "agentbox prune \-\-apply \-\-boxes \-\-running \-\-state \-\-images" .
.TP
.B doctor
Preflight: backends, KVM, kernel features, config validity, workspace
trust, SSH remotes, image staleness.
.TP
.BR trust " [" \-\-show "] [" \-\-revoke ]
Trust the workspace configuration. A committed
.B agentbox.toml
is executed by whoever clones the repository, so host-side
.B [hooks]
and
.B [[workspace.mounts]]
are refused until the file has been reviewed and trusted. Any edit to the
file (or to a file it extends) invalidates trust.
.B \-\-show
prints the current trust status;
.B \-\-revoke
withdraws it.
.TP
.BI "completion " SHELL
Print a completion script for
.BR bash ", " zsh ", or " fish .
.TP
.BR version " [" \-\-json ]
Tool version and backend versions.
.PP
.BR config ", " mounts ", " masks ", " backends ", and " doctor
never create or start anything. They are the supported way to answer "what
would this do" and "why can the agent see this file" before committing to a
session.
.SH GLOBAL FLAGS
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-directory " \fIDIR\fR"
Resolve the workspace as if from
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-workspace " \fIDIR\fR"
Override workspace-root detection.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " \fINAME\fR"
Address a box by instance name or ordinal alias (a decimal integer, ordered
by creation time).
.TP
.B \-\-new
Create an additional box rather than attaching to an existing one.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Apply
.BR stop ", " down ", or " status
to every box in the project.
.TP
.BI \-\-tree\-mode " MODE"
Override
.I [workspace].tree_mode
for a box being created:
.BR shared ", " worktree ", " copy ", or " auto .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-profile " \fINAME\fR"
Apply a named profile overlay.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIFILE\fR"
Use
.I FILE
as the workspace configuration layer.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Ignore discovered configuration; built-in defaults only.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-backend " \fINAME\fR"
Select a backend. Never selects one below the isolation floor.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-isolation " TIER"
Raise the isolation floor for this invocation. May raise, never lowers.
.TP
.BI \-\-force\-isolation " TIER"
Lower the floor deliberately. Recorded in the box's metadata, surfaced by
.BR status " and " ls ,
and warned about on every invocation of the resulting box.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-env " \fIKEY=VAL\fR"
Set a variable for this invocation (repeatable).
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-env\-passthrough " \fIKEY\fR"
Forward
.I KEY
from the caller's environment (repeatable).
.TP
.BI \-\-network " MODE"
Override
.IR [network].mode :
.BR none ", " proxy ", or " open .
.TP
.B \-\-offline
Equivalent to
.BR "\-\-network none" .
.TP
.B \-\-rebuild
Rebuild the image before starting.
.TP
.B \-\-recreate
Recreate the box before executing.
.TP
.B \-\-root
Execute as uid 0 in the guest. Under the container tier this is uid 0 with
all capabilities dropped; see
.BR agentbox-security (7).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mask
Skip masking. Warns on stderr on every invocation.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan and generated artifacts; change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output where supported.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet " / " \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Diagnostic verbosity.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
Startup wait budget (default 120).
.SH BOX SELECTION
Each project has a default box, initially the first created and changeable
with
.BR "agentbox default" .
Bare
.B agentbox
in a project with no boxes creates one; with boxes, it attaches to the
default; if the default was removed, it errors naming the available boxes
rather than guessing.
.B \-\-name
addresses a specific box, creating it only when
.B \-\-new
is also given.
.SH WORKSPACE DETECTION
First match wins: the
.B \-\-workspace
flag; the
.B AGENTBOX_WORKSPACE
environment variable; the nearest ancestor containing
.B agentbox.toml
or
.BR .agentbox.toml ;
the nearest ancestor containing a VCS root marker
.RB ( .git ", " .hg ", " .jj ", " .svn );
the starting directory. The search stops at
.B $HOME
and at filesystem boundaries, and refuses to select
.B /
or
.B $HOME
itself.
.SH STREAMS, SIGNALS, EXIT STATUS
Diagnostics go to stderr; stdout belongs to the command in the box, so
.B "agentbox cat build.json | jq ."
works unchanged. A TTY is allocated only when both stdin and stdout are
terminals.
.BR SIGINT ", " SIGTERM ", " SIGQUIT ", and " SIGWINCH
are forwarded to the process in the box.
.PP
The in-box command's exit code is returned verbatim. agentbox's own
failures use codes 64\(en78 per
.BR sysexits.h :
.TP
.B 69
No backend satisfies the isolation floor.
.TP
.B 77
The workspace configuration declares hooks or host mounts and is not
trusted; see
.BR trust .
.TP
.B 78
Configuration invalid, including backend-scope violations.
.PP
A command legitimately exiting 69 is indistinguishable from an unavailable
backend; callers needing certainty should use
.BR \-\-json ,
which reports tool status and command status separately.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B agentbox.toml, .agentbox.toml
Workspace configuration, at the workspace root. See
.BR agentbox.toml (5).
.TP
.B .agentignore
Mask patterns, gitignore syntax.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/agentbox/config.toml
User configuration.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/agentbox/agentignore
User-global mask patterns.
.TP
.B /etc/agentbox/config.toml
System configuration.
.TP
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/agentbox/projects/
Per-project state: box metadata, generated artifacts, worktrees, locks,
logs, trust records.
.TP
.B $XDG_CACHE_HOME/agentbox/
Generated build contexts and image records.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR agentbox.toml (5),
.BR agentbox-security (7),
.BR gitignore (5)
